// defn-scope-divergence.bml — can a defn see the let it was written beside?
//
// preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk
//
// A top-level `let`, then a top-level `defn` that reads it. Three sibling kernels close over
// the binding and answer 107. The runtime refuses the name before it runs:
//
//     [unbound-name] 'outer' in value position matched no binding/const/fn -- typo, missing
//     prelude, or a name from an enclosing scope a defn frame cannot see?
//
// The same shape ONE FRAME DEEPER agrees everywhere: a let inside a defn body, read by a defn
// declared in that body, answers 5 on all of them. So nothing about closing over a binding is
// itself the trouble. The top-level frame alone is where they part.
//
// FOUND BY BEING WRONG ABOUT IT. Writing observe/rag-crossfade-body.fk I hit that diagnostic,
// passed the values down by hand, and wrote in the receipt that fkwu "says so in one line,
// which is the right shape" — naming a design where three kernels disagree, without asking
// them. The body's own word for that is the modest false limit of row 1398: a limit is a
// MEASUREMENT, and I made it a mood. This cell is the measurement I owed.
//
// WITNESSED 2026-09-10 on this M4 Max:
//     top-level let   fkwu refuses     go 107   rust 107
//     inner let       fkwu 5           go 5     rust 5
//
// THE BODY HAS ALREADY PAID FOR THIS TWICE, believing it a runtime rule. PR #576 (2026-09-04)
// found a top-level `(let INQUIRY (bp ...))` invisible inside the nested defn that read it, and
// the identical shape three times over in edge-categories.fk. It called that a bug, confirmed it
// distinct from #573's defn-in-defn capture fix, and worked around it by converting each `let`
// into a niladic `defn` with every reference site turned into a call — the shape inquiry.fk
// carries today at line 21. What nobody did was ask the other kernels. They do not refuse. So
// cells are being written in a shape chosen to satisfy one arm, and the choice reads as Form's
// grammar rather than as one kernel's gap.
//
// WHICH ONE IS RIGHT IS NOT SETTLED HERE, and the shape of the disagreement says why it is
// worth settling rather than picking: the runtime made top-level lets EAGER at their position
// (the tailkeep heal), which is what lets a top-level statement run where it stands. A defn is
// not a statement that runs; it is a name that will run later, and the two readings of "later"
// are exactly what differ. Either the runtime grows the closure and a cell written the ordinary
// way stops refusing, or the siblings drop it and a cell that runs on three kernels stops
// running — and until one is taken, a cell that reads a top-level let is portable in only one
// direction, silently.
//
// THE STRAY LINE IN THE REPORT IS THE KERNEL SPEAKING. Once the refusing unit has been compiled
// its `.sym` remembers the refusal, and a later run says so on stderr rather than answering from
// a cache it knows is bad. It is not suppressed here. A day spent on partial readings dressed as
// whole ones is a poor day to start hiding a kernel telling the truth about itself.
//
// Run it:  ./fkwu observe/defn-scope-divergence.bml
section [form.bml] {
    class DefnScopeDivergence {
        def dsd-top() = "observe/fixtures/defn-sees-top-level-let.fk";
        def dsd-inner() = "observe/fixtures/defn-sees-inner-let.fk";
        def dsd-core() = "form/form-stdlib/core.fk";
        def dsd-refused() = -1;

        def dsd-present?(path) = if ge(file_size(path), 0) then 1 else 0;
        // a kernel that refuses prints a diagnostic, not a number; str_to_int reads 0 from it,
        // and 0 is not an answer this pair can produce, so it stands for the refusal.
        def dsd-ask(cmd) = str_to_int(host-exec(cmd, ""));
        def dsd-read(binary, unit) = dsd-ask(str_concat(binary, str_concat(" ", str_concat(dsd-core(), str_concat(" ", unit)))));
        def dsd-read-self(unit) = dsd-ask(str_concat("./fkwu ", unit));

        def dsd-word(v, want) = if eq(v, want) then "closes-over" else if eq(v, 0) then "refuses" else "other";
        def dsd-row(name, top, inner) = list(name, dsd-word(top, 107), dsd-word(inner, 5), top, inner);
        def dsd-name(r) = nth(r, 0);
        def dsd-top-word(r) = nth(r, 1);
        def dsd-inner-word(r) = nth(r, 2);

        def dsd-line(r) = print_str(str_concat("  ", str_concat(dsd-name(r), str_concat("  top-level ", str_concat(dsd-top-word(r), str_concat("   inner ", dsd-inner-word(r)))))));
        def dsd-lines-go(said, rest) = dsd-lines(rest);
        def dsd-lines(rs) = if eq(len(rs), 0) then 0 else dsd-lines-go(dsd-line(head(rs)), tail(rs));

        def dsd-sibling(name, binary) = if eq(dsd-present?(binary), 0) then list(name, "not-built", "not-built", -1, -1) else dsd-row(name, dsd-read(binary, dsd-top()), dsd-read(binary, dsd-inner()));
        def dsd-readings() = list(
            dsd-row("fkwu", dsd-read-self(dsd-top()), dsd-read-self(dsd-inner())),
            dsd-sibling("go  ", "form/form-kernel-go/bin-go"),
            dsd-sibling("rust", "form/form-kernel-rust/target/release/form-kernel-rust"));

        def dsd-count(rs, want) = if eq(len(rs), 0) then 0 else add(if str_eq(dsd-top-word(head(rs)), want) then 1 else 0, dsd-count(tail(rs), want));
        def dsd-report() {
            let said = print_str("defn-scope-divergence: can a defn see the let it was written beside?");
            let rs = dsd-readings();
            let body = dsd-lines(rs);
            let closes = dsd-count(rs, "closes-over");
            let refuses = dsd-count(rs, "refuses");
            let tail-line = print_str(str_concat("  top-level: closes-over=", str_concat(int_to_str(closes), str_concat(" refuses=", int_to_str(refuses)))));
            add(mul(closes, 10), refuses);
        }
    }
    dsd-report();
}
